--TEST--
A socket whose URL no scripted peer answers throws naming it and reaches nothing, exactly as an unanswered outbound call does — `rule:testing/an-outbound-socket-is-answered-by-a-scripted-peer`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other half of the keystone: a test that forgot to script a peer fails
// saying so rather than opening a connection to whatever the URL names. The
// refusal is a `LogicError` because it is a mistake in the test rather than a
// verdict on anything outside it, which is the reading its twin over a call
// already has.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/v1", ["scripted"]);

try {
    var $stray = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://elsewhere.example.com/v1");
} catch (LogicError $unscripted) {
    echo $unscripted->message, "\n";
}

// A prefix row answers every URL under it, and the exact one still wins where
// both could: the matching is the table's own, so a peer and an answer cannot
// come to disagree about which row a URL selects.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/*", ["by prefix"]);
var $under = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/v2/feed");
var $exact = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/v1");
echo "prefix ", $under->receive()?->text() ?? "-", "\n";
echo "exact ", $exact->receive()?->text() ?? "-", "\n";

// Nothing was sent, because nothing connected: the record is empty rather than
// carrying the frames of a socket that failed to open.
echo "sent=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Test::sentSocket()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Http\Client::openSocket: this test answers outbound sockets from a table and no peer is registered for wss://elsewhere.example.com/v1 — `Core\Test::answerSocket` registers one, exactly or as a prefix ending in `*`
prefix by prefix
exact scripted
sent=0
